Chapter: DiskFactsMultiLoad
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Kernel chapter AppPersist
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The boot-time replay walks the fact log and rebuilds the in-memory store.
 It has to survive two things this test puts in front of it.

 A fact is not one sector. pack-fact-entry has always sized itself with
 sectors-for-fact, and app-write-fact -- the writer the repository protocol
 uses -- writes as many sectors as the content needs. A source definition is
 thousands of bytes. The replay used to read a single sector, refuse anything
 longer, and return the store it had built so far, which ended the walk. One
 multi-sector fact truncated the whole store at that entry: every fact behind
 it sat on the disk, intact and verifiable, and was invisible. The superblock
 kept reporting the full count, so nothing looked wrong.

 A corrupt fact ended the walk for the same reason. The honest response to one
 bad entry is to skip that entry, not to discard every entry behind it.

 So: write a 960-byte fact, then two small ones behind it, then flip a byte of
 the big one's content so its stored hash no longer matches what it holds. The
 big fact must be rejected -- content addressing is worth nothing if it is not
 -- and both small facts must survive, which they can only do if the walk
 strode over the whole multi-sector entry and carried on.

Section: Entry

  rep : Integer, Text, Text -> Text
  rep (n) (chunk) (acc) =
   if n <= 0 then acc else rep (n - 1) chunk (acc & chunk)

  big : Text = rep 60 "0123456789abcdef" ""

  shown : Maybe Fact, Text -> Text
  shown (m) (want) =
   when m
    is Just (f) -> if f.content == want then "ok" else "CORRUPTED"
    is None -> "LOST"

  rejected : Maybe Fact -> Text
  rejected (m) =
   when m
    is Just (f) -> "NO -- ACCEPTED"
    is None -> "yes"

  corrupt-content-byte : Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  corrupt-content-byte (sector) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector sector
    let old = peek-byte buf fact-entry-header-size
    in let w = poke-byte buf fact-entry-header-size (if old == 88 then 89 else 88)
    in block-write-sector sector buf
  end

  opening : [Device.Block, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let store = disk-init
    in let s1 = app-write-fact store 30 big 1
    in let s2 = disk-write-fact s1 DefinitionFact "hello" 2
    in let s3 = disk-write-fact s2 PolicyFact "world" 3
    in let flushed = disk-checkpoint s3
    in act
      print-line-uni ("facts written: " & show (disk-fact-count flushed))
      let clean = disk-load
      in let big-clean = disk-read-fact clean (fact-content-hash big)
      in print-line-uni ("multi-sector fact of " & show (text-length big) & " bytes: " & shown big-clean big)
      damaged <- corrupt-content-byte fact-log-start
      let after = disk-load
      in let big-bad = disk-read-fact after (fact-content-hash big)
      in let h = disk-read-fact after (fact-content-hash "hello")
      in let w = disk-read-fact after (fact-content-hash "world")
      in act
        print-line-uni ("corrupted multi-sector fact rejected: " & rejected big-bad)
        print-line-uni ("facts behind it survive the skip: " & shown h "hello" & " " & shown w "world")
      end
    end
  end

